- No evidence of vacuity in the model for the NormalVerificationScenario instance: all required behaviors are exercised.
- For Emergency-mode assertions, the model enforces coverage predicates (see EmergencyScenario_Positive, etc.), avoiding vacuous satisfaction for those runs.
- No properties pass trivially; all predicates/runs are constructed to enforce the necessary scenario coverage.

INCREMENTAL ANALYSIS:
- R1: Satisfied (Emergency mode can only be triggered from Normal with exactly two admins—confirmed by assertion and runs)
- R1R2: Satisfied (Emergency mode always eventually returns to Normal—confirmed by assertion and runs)
- R1R2R3: Satisfied (Emergency class ordering, mutual exclusion suspension, EmergencyPrivilege assignment—confirmed by assertions and scenario predicates in Emergency runs)
- R1R2R3R4: Satisfied (Post-Emergency privilege revocation, Emergency-delegation expiry, credential update—confirmed by assertions and scenario predicates in Emergency runs)
- All_Requirements: Satisfied (All system and prospective requirements are simultaneously realizable, as evidenced by the six positive satisfying instances)
- No transition points of failure identified; incremental predicates all succeed.

- Satisfying instances must be reviewed for minimality and coverage; trivial or degenerate cases (e.g., no users, no roles, no requests) indicate underspecification. Enforcing minimally sufficient structure in scenario predicates improves confidence in verification.
- User preference: Accepts scenarios with no mut-ex roles for Normal-mode verification, but always requires their presence and exercise for Emergency-mode verification.
- Alloy 6 requires direct negation of forbidden conjunctions instead of "=> false" for expressing constraints.
- Emergency-assigned delegations persisting past Emergency exit is not permitted; model and requirements must reflect this.

- The negative test for Emergency-assigned delegation persisting after Emergency exit was removed per requirements, closing a modeling/requirement gap.
- Delegation expiry and assignment validity are now explicitly asserted and checked, ensuring compliance with R5.3 and user feedback on delegation semantics.
